    "https://www.uwa.edu/the-university-of-west-alabama-seeks-an-associate-provost-for-academic-affairs/": {
      "title": "The University of West Alabama seeks an Associate Provost for Academic Affairs",
      "description": "The University of West Alabama seeks an Associate Provost for Academic Affairs | University of West Alabama It's Free Application Week! Apply to UWA for FREE, October 5–9! Application fees are waived for all online, on-campus, undergraduate, and graduate applicants. Apply for Free Skip to content More Schedule a Tour Apply Now Athletics Alumni TigerHub Portal UWA Official Bookstore Schedule a Tour Request Information A-Z Index Students Academics About UWA Admissions & Aid Life at UWA Online Programs Give Back Apply Now The University of West Alabama seeks an Associate Provost for Academic Affairs Home » The University of West Alabama seeks an Associate Provost for Academic Affairs View Position Profile Location Livingston, Alabama The Position The Associate Provost for Academic Affairs provides senior-level academic leadership and operational support to the Provost in furthering the academic mission, strategic priorities, and institutional goals of the University. This position serves as a principal advisor, strategic partner, and operational leader for academic quality, faculty support, student achievement, academic integrity, and continuous improvement across Academic Affairs. Working collaboratively with academic, campus, and community partners, the Associate Provost leads and supports initiatives, strengthens communication across units, promotes effective academic operations, and helps align Academic Affairs with the University’s strategic plan and regional mission. The Associate Provost manages priorities, solves problems, communicates effectively, and exercises sound judgment and discretion to move complex work forward across academic and administrative units. Responsibilities Provide administrative, academic, and operational support to the Provost in advancing the priorities and daily work of Academic Affairs. Serve as a trusted strategic and operational partner to the Provost by helping coordinate work across academic units, support offices, committees, university leadership, and external partners as appropriate. Coordinate the planning and implementation of assigned academic initiatives, policies and procedures, special projects, and cross-unit efforts in support of institutional priorities and the University’s strategic plan. Demonstrate proactive leadership by identifying needs, resolving barriers, communicating next steps, and ensuring timely follow-through on assigned responsibilities. Provide leadership, oversight, and direction for assigned Academic Affairs functions, areas, programs, services, and initiatives. Promote a culture of academic excellence by supporting high-quality teaching, effective learning practices, meaningful assessment, faculty development, program improvement, and continuous enhancement of academic programs and services. Develop and oversee the Center for Teaching and Learning Excellence as a resource for faculty development, instructional innovation, academic quality, student learning, and excellence in teaching. Support faculty onboarding, orientation, professional development, mentoring, and related initiatives that promote effective teaching, faculty engagement, academic quality, and faculty success across Academic Affairs. Advance student access and success by identifying barriers, strengthening academic pathways, improving coordination of academic support, and promoting practices that serve all students. Provide leadership for initiatives that promote academic integrity, ethical academic practice, and consistent implementation of academic policies, including education and support for faculty, staff, and students. Collaborate with deans, department chairs, faculty leaders, academic support units, Student Affairs, Enrollment Management, institutional effectiveness, and other campus partners to advance shared priorities and strengthen coordination across Academic Affairs. Use data, assessment findings, student success indicators, feedback, and institutional priorities to identify needs, recommend improvements, and support effective decision-making across assigned areas. Support academic initiatives that contribute to workforce development, community engagement, educational attainment, and quality of life in the rural region served by the University. Help ensure that academic programs, faculty development efforts, academic success initiatives, and operational improvements align with the University’s mission, strategic plan, accreditation expectations, and institutional priorities. Lead and manage assigned projects, requests, meetings, and planning efforts by tracking timelines, coordinating next steps, gathering information, resolving barriers, and ensuring timely follow-up and completion. Identify operational needs, process gaps, and opportunities for improved coordination, communication, efficiency, and effectiveness across Academic Affairs. Support the implementation of academic policies, procedures, initiatives, and administrative processes in collaboration with appropriate university offices, ensuring accountability for timely, accurate, and consistent execution. Assist with sensitive, confidential, and time-sensitive matters requiring sound judgment, discretion, professionalism, and attention to detail. Represent the Office of the Provost in meetings, committees, events, community conversations, and cross-functional projects as assigned. Complete other duties as assigned in support of the Office of the President, Provost, Academic Affairs, and broader university priorities. Key Leadership Areas The Associate Provost for Academic Affairs is expected to advance work in the following areas: Academic Excellence: Strengthening teaching, learning, faculty development, academic quality, assessment, innovation, and continuous improvement. Academic Integrity: Promoting ethical academic practice, consistent policy implementation, accountability, and a culture of honesty and trust in teaching and learning. Student Access and Success: Supporting pathways that improve access, persistence, completion, and student achievement, particularly for students whose success depends on coordinated academic support. Regional and Rural Impact: Helping ensure that academic programs and initiatives respond to regional needs, workforce priorities, community partnerships, and the University’s responsibility to serve its region. Strategic Plan Alignment: Connecting academic initiatives, operations, policies, and resource decisions to the University’s strategic goals and measurable outcomes. Qualifications Minimum Earned doctorate or terminal degree from an accredited institution. Progressively responsible leadership background in academic affairs administration. Demonstrated ability to lead or coordinate academic initiatives, policies, programs, and projects across multiple units. Demonstrated commitment to academic excellence, academic integrity, student access and achievement, accountability, and continuous improvement. Strong communication, collaboration, organizational, analytical, and problem-solving skills. Ability to exercise sound judgment, discretion, professionalism, and confidentiality. Preferred Academic leadership as a dean, assistant/associate provost, vice provost, provost, or in a comparable role. Teaching experience as a faculty member in higher education, with a demonstrated commitment to effective teaching and student learning. Background in faculty development, teaching and learning initiatives, academic success initiatives, and/or accreditation. Demonstrated success advancing student achievement, access, persistence, retention, and completion. Experience supporting academic workforce alignment, community partnerships, regional engagement, or strategic plan implementation. About The University of West Alabama The University of West Alabama (UWA) was chartered in 1835 as a church-related female academy and remained primarily a school for girls until the twentieth century. 2025 marked 190 years of proud tradition and educational excellence with more than 41,000 graduates. The University of West Alabama is accredited by the Southern Association of Colleges and Schools Commission on Colleges (SACSCOC) to award associate, bachelor, master, education specialist, and doctoral degrees through the College of Business, Julia Tutwiler College of Education, College of Liberal Arts, College of Natural Sciences and Mathematics, School of Engineering Technology, Ira D. Pruitt College of Nursing and Health Professions, School of Graduate Studies, and Online Programs. Explore buildings, parking, accessibility resources, and other campus locations using the UWA Interactive Campus Map . Mission Enriching lives through education, service, and outreach. Vision To offer quality education and effective services while establishing a model for leadership in the region and beyond. Strategic Plan The University of West Alabama’s Strategic Plan 2026–2035, Your Bridge to What’s Possible , outlines a bold vision for advancing student success, access, and institutional growth in rural and regional higher education. The plan prioritizes enrollment expansion, retention, and a personalized student experience supported by flexible learning models, strong partnerships, and data-informed decision-making. Grounded in values of integrity, excellence, collaboration, and regional responsibility, UWA is committed to serving as a catalyst for opportunity and prosperity in the communities it serves. Review the University’s goals, priorities, and vision for the future in the UWA Strategic Plan 2026–2035 (PDF) Points of Interest A total of 30 new academic programs have been added over the past 6 years, including 2 doctoral programs (Ed.D. & D.B.A.) Over 100 international students on campus from 41 different countries UWA has been consistently recognized as one of the safest, most diverse, and best value of any public university in the state UWA was awarded the Governor’s Seal of Excellence in Work-Based Learning for its Integrated Marketing Communications Program The Division of Economic & Workforce Development received the American Association of State Colleges and Universities (AASCU) Excellence and Innovation Award for Regional and Economic Development In 2025, UWA was designated as an Opportunity College and University (OCU), reflecting its long-standing commitment to both access and economic outcomes for graduates In 2023, the Alabama Black Belt National Heritage Area was designated; UWA’s Center for the Study of the Black Belt serves as the management entity for this area The institution has received multiple national rankings for best online college, especially at the master’s level in education (e.g., 3rd in the nation for “Best Online Master’s Degrees in Secondary Education”) Community The University Charter School (UCS) on the University of West Alabama (UWA) campus had a significant milestone when it opened in 2018. Its mission is to offer a strong educational experience, particularly for students in rural areas, providing them with opportunities for academic growth and success. By May 2023, the school had graduated its first senior class, marking a major achievement in its development. The vision of UCS to serve as a model for rural education underlines its commitment to providing quality schooling in areas that may otherwise lack access to resources. Today, the School provides Pre-K through 12 th grade education, continues to receive excellent marks in the state’s annual review, and has been ranked No. 1 in Alabama Charter Elementary Schools among the 2024 Best Elementary School rankings by U.S. News & World Report. Leadership Dr. Amy H. Jones was appointed as Provost in May of this year, after serving as Interim Provost since October 2025. During her tenure as interim provost, she strengthened academic operations and infrastructure, enhanced the University’s ability to make informed, data-driven decisions, and advanced initiatives focused on faculty support, student success, and experiential learning opportunities. “Dr. Jones has demonstrated exceptional leadership and a deep commitment to the mission of UWA,” said UWA President Dr. Todd G. Fritch. “Her experience, vision, and collaborative approach position Academic Affairs for continued growth and excellence as we advance the goals of the University’s strategic plan.” Since joining the faculty in 2007 as an instructor, Jones has advanced through the faculty ranks and held numerous leadership roles, most recently serving as dean of the College of Liberal Arts. Throughout her career at UWA, she has contributed extensively to committees and initiatives supporting both faculty and student success. Upon her appointment, Jones said, “Education has the power to transform lives, and at UWA, that impact reaches beyond our students to strengthen the region we support. I am honored to have the opportunity to serve as provost and look forward to working collaboratively with our students, faculty, staff, and community partners to advance academic excellence, expand opportunity, and strengthen the University’s lasting impact.” Jones holds a doctorate in communication and information science from The University of Alabama, a Master of Science in sport administration from the University of Louisville, and a Bachelor of Arts in film, theatre and communication arts from the University of New Orleans. Dr. Todd G. Fritch is the 13th president of the University of West Alabama. His term began January 2, 2025. Fritch holds a Ph.D. in Geology from Baylor University, where he also earned his M.S. in Geology. He earned his B.S. in Geology, cum laude, from Lake Superior State University in Sault Ste. Marie, Michigan. Fritch completed both the Institute for Educational Management program and the Executive Certificate in Higher Education Leadership at the Harvard Graduate School of Education. Previously, Fritch served as Executive Vice President and Provost at the State College of Florida, Manatee-Sarasota, where he was instrumental in driving academic excellence and fostering a culture of inclusivity and engagement. Prior roles include serving as Chief Academic Officer and Vice President at Shorelight Education, and as Provost at American International College. Fritch has also held senior academic positions at The American College of Greece and Northeastern University. Throughout his career, Fritch has shown an unwavering dedication to student success, faculty growth, and community collaboration, and has a robust background in financial resource development. This expertise enables him to promote growth at UWA by utilizing his extensive experience in securing critical fiscal resources while working to develop and maintain valuable partnerships. Fritch has also been actively involved in numerous professional and community service roles, including serving on the Board of Directors as Vice Chair for the Sarasota County Economic Development Corporation; the Board of Governors for Manatee Technical College; the School Advisory Council for Suncoast Technical College; and as the Community Board Chair for the Bradenton YMCA. He has been recognized with several honors and awards, including the Chancellor’s Best Practice Award for Workforce Partnerships from the Florida Department of Education in 2019 and the Outstanding Alumni Award from Lake Superior State University in 2016. Since becoming president of UWA in January 2025, Fritch has led significant institutional growth and strategic investment, supported by a strong financial position and increasing state support. His administration has expanded academic and student-success initiatives through the expansion of the College of Nursing and Health Professions, revitalization of the University Honors Program, enhanced support for first- and second-year students, and development of the Center of Excellence in Conservation, Agriculture, and Sustainability that aligns resources and initiatives to strengthen the University’s ability to uniquely serve the Black Belt region. Fritch has also advanced comprehensive campus master planning, major investments in facilities and infrastructure, and new educational partnerships, including UWA’s collaborations with the Alabama School of Healthcare Sciences and the YMCA of Tuscaloosa. Dr. Fritch and his wife, Jamie Fritch, are active and engaged members of the University community, the Livingston community, and the greater West Alabama community. To Apply Acceptance of inquiries, nominations, and applications will begin immediately and continue until the completion of the search. To apply, please submit a CV and a cover letter that addresses your reasons for interest and related qualifications/experience. Applications will be reviewed as they are submitted. Interested parties are encouraged to apply no later than October 2, 2026 . Submit your application online through the UWA Associate Provost for Academic Affairs application . All applications and nominations will be held in confidence. For more information or to make a recommendation or nomination Caitlin Hummel (585) 880-7550 [email protected] Before hiring, the final candidate(s) must successfully pass a pre-employment background investigation, including information obtained from social media and other internet sources. The University of West Alabama does not discriminate on the basis of race, religion, hearing status, personal appearance, color, sex, pregnancy, political affiliation, creed, ethnicity, national origin (including ancestry), citizenship status, physical or mental disability (including perceived disability), age, marital status, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran or military status, predisposing genetic characteristics, domestic violence victim status, or any other protected category under applicable local, state, or federal law, including protections for those opposing discrimination or participating in any grievance process on campus, with the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission, or other human rights agencies. AA/EOE. Minority applications encouraged. Apply Now 100 N. Washington St.,\nLivingston,\nAL 35470 (205) 652-3400 Administration Americans with Disabilities Act A-Z Index Consumer Information Directory Employment Map & Directions Media Reset Your Password Self Service Parking and Traffic Regulations University News Weather © Copyright 2025 University of West Alabama. All rights reserved. Privacy Policy Public & Legal Notices Title IX Sitemap Schedule a Tour Athletics Alumni TigerHub Portal UWA Official Bookstore A-Z Index Search Students Back to Main Menu Prospective Students Accepted Students Go West Orientation Current Students Transfer Students International Students Online Students Family & Guardians Graduation and Commencement Back to Students Apply for Your Degree Event Details Regalia Diploma Information Annual Commencement Honors Map and Directions Watch Live Graduation & Commencement FAQs Academics Back to Main Menu Academics Overview Back to Academics Academics Overview Programs Online Programs Study Abroad Programs Academic Support College of Business College of Education College of Liberal Arts College of Natural Sciences and Mathematics College of Nursing and Health Professions School of Engineering Technology School of Graduate Studies University Honors Program Academic Resources Back to Academics Julia Tutwiler Library Academic Catalogs Academic Calendars Final Exam Schedule Transcript Request Student Rights and Records About UWA Back to Main Menu Mission & Vision History & Tradition Accreditations Fast Facts Office of the President Board of Trustees Employment Opportunities Caring Campus Initiative Virtual Campus Tour Faculty & Staff Directory University Departments Back to About UWA University Departments Registrar Educator Preparation & Certification Office Student Advocacy Julia Tutwiler Library Institutional Effectiveness & Strategic Planning Information Technology Student Affairs Univer"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/185756": {
      "title": "Graduate Assistant (GA)- Athletic Training",
      "description": "Department: University of West Alabama: Academic Affairs: College of Natural Sciences and Mathematics: School of Kinesiology and Human Performance Location: University of West Alabama The University of West Alabama Athletic Training and Sports Medicine Center invites applicants for multiple Graduate Assistant Athletic Trainer positions. Responsibilities: Assist with the CAATE (Commission on Accreditation of Athletic Training Education) accredited graduate Athletic Training Programs as a teaching assistant. Work autonomously with assigned sport(s), but have the ability to collaborate with the certified athletic trainers on staff as desired for input on evaluation, treatment, rehabilitation, etc. Provide athletic training services to patients from the community and surrounding areas of the UWA Athletic Training & Sports Medicine Center to experience a wide variety and large volume of pathology. Collaborate directly with team physicians, fellows, and specialists from Andrews Sports Medicine & Orthopedic Center. Provide full autonomy athletic training services to numerous secondary school athletes within our extensive outreach program. Supervise athletic training program students as their clinical preceptor. Salary & Benefits: Monthly stipend of $1,000 (August – April). Full out-of-state tuition waiver and scholarship coverage of all on-campus and online (if the same course is not offered on-campus). courses towards your desired master's degree. Coverage of course fees and textbook rentals. Opportunity for additional income from outreach events. Full reimbursement of the State of Alabama licensure each year. Full registration, hotel, and travel (with us) for the NATA Convention, SEATA Clinical Symposium & Members Meeting, ALATA Annual Meeting, and GSC AT Annual Meeting, plus the American Sports Medicine Institute Injuries in Baseball Meeting and the Andrews Institute Injuries in Football Meeting each year. UWA Athletic Training apparel and equipment, including, but not limited to, polo shirts, t-shirts, sweatshirts, rain gear, and necessary AT equipment (most varsity sports will provide some additional clothing). About the UWA Athletic Training & Sports Medicine Center: The University of West Alabama Athletic Training & Sports Medicine Center was established in 1986 to organize and formalize efforts toward improvement of athletic health care in West Alabama. The Center provides athletic training services primarily to UWA athletics and the University faculty, staff, and students. Secondarily, it focuses on providing athletic training services to secondary school athletics and the West Alabama/East Mississippi area. Patients include those with musculoskeletal and orthopedic concerns ranging from pediatrics to the geriatric population. The Center is staffed primarily by 14 full-time certified athletic trainers, three graduate assistant athletic trainers, and approximately 20 athletic training students. Support personnel includes fellowship-trained orthopedic surgeons and non-surgical orthopedic physicians, physical therapists, exercise physiologists, biomechanists, health & physical educators, and others in related areas. Go to http://at.uwa.edu/ for more information. Required · Bachelor’s Degree (Athletic Training, Health, Physical Education, and/or related area preferred). · Certification (or eligibility to be certified) by the Board of Certification. · Ability to clinically supervise students in the CAATE-accredited athletic training graduate program and preprofessional athletic training students while providing athletic training services to intercollegiate and interscholastic athletics. · Ability to work effectively with the administration, faculty, staff, and students within the university. Preferred · Athletic training experience in collegiate and high school athletics. Before hiring, the final candidate(s) must successfully pass a pre-employment background investigation, including information obtained from social media and other internet sources. Review of applicants is happening NOW!  To join our team where athletic training is a priority, see below: To Apply: When you select \"Apply Now,\" you will be prompted to provide the following: Cover Letter. A current resume or CV. Transcripts of all college work. Unofficial transcripts will suffice for application.  Official transcripts must be submitted immediately upon employment. Email and phone number of three professional references.  Reference letters may be requested later."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193519": {
      "title": "Director of Assessment and Accreditation, Julia S. Tutwiler College of Education",
      "description": "Department: University of West Alabama: Academic Affairs: College of Education Location: University of West Alabama The Director of Assessment and Accreditation provides strategic leadership for assessment, accreditation, data reporting, and continuous improvement within the Julia S. Tutwiler College of Education. Reporting to the Dean of the College of Education, the Director leads the College’s quality assurance and assessment processes and coordinates activities necessary to maintain compliance with institutional, state, federal, and professional accreditation requirements. The Director promotes a culture of continuous improvement by translating assessment and accreditation requirements into effective processes and supporting faculty and leadership in using evidence to strengthen programs and candidate outcomes. Key Responsibilities Accreditation Leadership & Compliance Serve as the College’s primary resource and coordinator for accreditation through the Council for the Accreditation of Educator Preparation (CAEP), including accreditation planning, annual reporting, self-study development, evidence collection, accreditation reviews, and site visits. Coordinate College-level activities associated with state, federal, institutional, and specialized accreditation and reporting requirements, including requirements of the Alabama State Department of Education (ALSDE), Specialized Professional Associations (SPAs), CACREP, and other programmatic accreditors, SACSCOC, and applicable federal reporting requirements. Develop and maintain multi-year accreditation plans and an annual assessment and accreditation calendar to ensure timely completion of reporting, program review, assessment, survey administration, and accreditation activities. Maintain a comprehensive and organized repository of accreditation evidence, assessment results, reports, and continuous improvement documentation to ensure ongoing accreditation readiness. Coordinate preparation for accreditation and program review visits, including timelines, evidence preparation and verification, stakeholder preparation, review activities, and follow-up requirements. Data Analysis & Continuous Improvement Analyze and communicate assessment and performance data to identify trends, strengths, areas of concern, and opportunities for improvement. Facilitate systematic review and interpretation of data with college leadership, departments, programs, and faculty. Assist faculty and program coordinators in using assessment findings to inform curricular, instructional, clinical, and programmatic decisions and documenting resulting continuous improvement efforts. Develop reports, summaries, presentations, and other data products that clearly communicate candidate performance, program outcomes, and EPP effectiveness to internal and external stakeholders. Provide data and analysis to support College strategic planning, program evaluation, decision-making, and evaluation of progress toward established goals. Collaboration & Strategic Support Serve as a strategic advisor to the Dean and College leadership regarding assessment, accreditation, educator preparation accountability, program quality, and emerging state and national expectations affecting educator preparation. Directly work with College of Education Program Coordinators on annual reporting, data requests, and other items related to assessment and accreditation. Collaborate with the Office of Institutional Effectiveness and Research, Academic Affairs, Information Technology, and other University offices to obtain, validate, analyze, and report data necessary for assessment and accreditation. Work collaboratively with department chairs, program coordinators, faculty, and staff to ensure assessment and accreditation responsibilities are effectively communicated and completed. Utilize institutional systems, including Colleague and Blackboard, and other appropriate technologies to support data collection, analysis, reporting, and continuous improvement processes. Represent the College on appropriate University committees and at state, regional, and national meetings related to assessment, accreditation, educator preparation, and institutional effectiveness. Maintain current knowledge of accreditation standards, assessment practices, educator preparation policy, data reporting requirements, and emerging trends affecting colleges of education. Perform other related duties as assigned by the Dean of the College of Education or other appropriate University administrators. FLSA: Exempt Before hiring, the final candidate(s) must successfully pass a pre-employment background investigation, including information obtained from social media and other internet sources. Required Qualifications Master’s degree in education, Higher Education, Educational Leadership, Assessment/Evaluation, Research, or a related field. Demonstrated experience in assessment, accreditation, institutional effectiveness, data analysis/reporting, educator preparation, or a related area. Demonstrated ability to collect, manage, analyze, interpret, and communicate quantitative and qualitative data. Strong understanding of assessment, program evaluation, data-informed decision-making, and continuous improvement practices. Demonstrated ability to manage complex projects, reporting cycles, and multiple deadlines involving a variety of stakeholders. Excellent written, oral, organizational, project management, and collaborative skills. Demonstrated ability to use technology and information systems for data collection, management, analysis, and reporting. Preferred Qualifications Doctoral degree in Education, Higher Education, Educational Leadership, Assessment/Evaluation, Research, or a related field. Direct experience with the Council for the Accreditation of Educator Preparation (CAEP), including accreditation preparation, annual reporting, self-study development, evidence management, or accreditation review. Experience working within an educator preparation provider (EPP), College or School of Education, or similar academic environment. Experience with specialized professional accreditation or program review processes, including CACREP or other professional accrediting organizations. Experience developing, implementing, maintaining, or evaluating a comprehensive assessment or quality assurance system. Experience facilitating faculty use of assessment data for program evaluation and continuous improvement. Experience preparing accreditation reports, self-studies, evidence repositories, or materials for accreditation and program review visits. Familiarity with student information systems and learning management systems, including Colleague and Blackboard. To Apply: When you select \"Apply Now,\" you will be prompted to provide the following: Cover Letter. A current resume or CV. Transcripts of all college work. Unofficial transcripts will suffice for application.  Official transcripts must be submitted immediately upon employment. Email and phone number of three professional references.  Reference letters may be requested later."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192517": {
      "title": "Graduate Assistant (GA)- Julia Tutwiler Library",
      "description": "Department: University of West Alabama: Academic Affairs: Julia Tutwiler Library Location: University of West Alabama Make a difference at The University of West Alabama as a Graduate Assistant for Julia Tutwiler Library. This position supports the staff of the Julia Tutwiler Library in the day-to-day operations of the library, as the whole team works together to fulfill the mission of the library. Ideal candidates are service-minded, collaborative, critical thinkers, great communicators, and detail-oriented. Assist in the work of the library, connecting patrons to information and services needed to fulfill their academic endeavors. Help create a welcoming library environment. Preserve information for future generations. Use skills that you are gaining in your graduate program in the dynamic environment of The University of West Alabama Julia Tutwiler Library. Job duties will include: Assist library patrons with finding materials, printing, and scheduling appointments with librarians. Use the library management system, Alma and Primo VE, to assist library patrons and complete projects. Pull library holds and assist with interlibrary loan and resource sharing. Gather and analyze information, often creating or using spreadsheets. Support library staff in their work. Understand and apply the Dewey Decimal Classification System. Ability to lift 30 pounds. The successful candidate will be assigned to special projects as needed. Work flexible hours, including evenings and occasional weekends, as required. Part-time, 9-month position, with a $1,000 monthly stipend. This position is not eligible for benefits. Required: Bachelor's Degree from an accredited university. Experience in a service-oriented setting. Must be enrolled full-time and in good standing with a UWA graduate program. Prior to hiring, the final candidate(s) must successfully pass a pre-employment background investigation, including information obtained from social media and other internet sources. To Apply: When you select \"Apply Now,\" you will be prompted to provide the following: Cover Letter. A current resume or CV. Transcripts of all college work. Unofficial transcripts will suffice for application.  Official transcripts must be submitted immediately upon employment. Email and phone number of three professional references.  Reference letters may be requested later."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192711": {
      "title": "Administrative Support Assistant, Registrar's Office",
      "description": "Department: University of West Alabama: Academic Affairs: Registrar's Office Location: University of West Alabama Essential Duties and Responsibilities Maintain the office and perform administrative duties, including answering and directing phone calls, monitoring the Registrar's Department email, ordering and maintaining office supplies and materials (including transcript paper), and supporting the day-to-day operations of the department. Serve as the first point of contact for students, faculty, staff, and community members. Process transcript requests. Register students. Process drop and add forms. Process enrollment verifications and in-school deferments. Enter AIC holds. Process name and address changes. Manage the change of major process. Train and supervise student workers. Process the Apostille documents for international students. Maintain a continual knowledge of all degrees offered and the course requirements for each program. Other duties as determined by the Registrar. Cross-Departmental Duties Assist with the entry of the Master Class Schedule. Assist with the residency reclassification process. Assist in marking student's deceased in the system. Other duties determined by the Registrar. FLSA: Non-Exempt Essential: Bachelor's degree from an accredited institution. Minimum of two (2) years of experience in office-related work. Excellent communication, organizational, and problem-solving skills. Desired: Master's degree (or higher) from an accredited college or university. Experience with Ellucian Colleague or a similar student information system (SIS). Before hiring, the final candidate(s) must successfully pass a pre-employment background investigation, including information obtained from social media and other internet sources. To Apply: When you select \"Apply Now,\" you will be prompted to provide the following: Cover Letter. A current resume or CV. Transcripts of all college work. Unofficial transcripts will suffice for the application.  Official transcripts must be submitted immediately upon employment. Email and phone number of three professional references.  Reference letters may be requested later."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191815": {
      "title": "Director of Instructional Design",
      "description": "Department: University of West Alabama: Academic Affairs: Division of Online Programs Location: University of West Alabama The Director of Instructional Design provides operational leadership for the design, development, and quality assurance of academic courses across all colleges and modalities. Reporting to the Dean of Online Programs, the Director ensures that course design processes, faculty onboarding, and instructional development initiatives align with institutional standards and strengthen both teaching effectiveness and student learning experiences. This position operationalizes the university’s vision for academic excellence by coordinating instructional design workflows, establishing and maintaining course quality standards, leading comprehensive faculty onboarding and professional development initiatives, and supporting student readiness to ensure a smooth and well-supported start to their academic programs. Key Responsibilities Instructional Design Leadership Oversee the design, development, and revision of academic courses to ensure alignment with institutional quality standards and accreditation expectations. Lead, supervise, and mentor the Instructional Design team to ensure consistent and high-quality instructional support. Establish and manage workflows, timelines, and priorities for course development and operations across all colleges and instructional modalities. Collaborate with academic leadership, program coordinators, and faculty to ensure effective course mapping, learning outcomes alignment, and assessment design. Manage and coordinate instructional design support provided through our online program management (OPM) partner to ensure alignment with institutional priorities, timelines, and quality standards. Meet regularly with department chairs and deans to discuss instructional design standards, processes, and procedures, and identify instructional needs and course improvement priorities. Faculty Onboarding & Professional Development Lead and manage the faculty onboarding process, ensuring new instructors are prepared to teach effectively and meet academic expectations. Develop and deliver ongoing training, workshops, and resources that support faculty in pedagogical best practices, instructional technology, and course design standards. Partner and meet with department chairs and deans to ensure faculty receive timely and relevant instructional support. Evaluate areas for faculty development and recommend strategies to strengthen teaching quality and instructional consistency. Quality Assurance & Continuous Improvement Maintain and refine course quality standards, review processes, and evaluation tools. Oversee routine course audits and collaborate with faculty to implement improvements. Monitor trends and emerging best practices in instructional design and pedagogy to inform institutional strategy. Ensure accessibility standards, usability guidelines, and compliance requirements are consistently met across all courses. Student Readiness & Support Contribute to student onboarding processes by coordinating instructional components that promote readiness and early engagement. Collaborate with academic and student success units to ensure course design supports student navigation, clarity, and early success. Provide insight into student learning needs to inform course improvements and instructional support strategies. Collaboration & Strategic Support Work closely with the Dean of Online Programs on initiatives related to academic quality, innovative teaching, and learning support. Partner with academic and administrative units to support institutional goals for student success, retention, and instructional excellence. Assist in data-informed decision-making by analyzing trends related to faculty preparedness, course quality, and instructional support needs. Perform other related duties as assigned by the Dean of Online Programs or other appropriate University administrators. FLSA: Exempt Before hiring, the final candidate(s) must successfully pass a pre-employment background investigation, including information obtained from social media and other internet sources. Required Qualifications Master’s degree in Instructional Design, Education, Curriculum & Instruction, Educational Technology, or a related field. Demonstrated experience in instructional design, curriculum development, or faculty development in a higher education setting. Supervisory experience or demonstrated leadership within an academic or instructional design team. Strong understanding of pedagogical best practices, learning theory, and assessment design. Excellent project management, communication, and collaborative skills. Preferred Qualifications Doctoral degree in a related field. Experience leading faculty onboarding or instructional development programs. Experience with quality assurance frameworks such as Quality Matters or equivalent. Familiarity with accessibility standards, universal design for learning (UDL), and relevant compliance requirements. Demonstrated ability to support course design across multiple modalities. Before hiring, the final candidate(s) must successfully pass a pre-employment background investigation, including information obtained from social media and other internet sources. To Apply: When you select \"Apply Now,\" you will be prompted to provide the following: Cover Letter. A current resume or CV. Transcripts of all college work. Unofficial transcripts will suffice for application.  Official transcripts must be submitted immediately upon employment. Email and phone number of three professional references.  Reference letters may be requested later."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194594": {
      "title": "PC Technician",
      "description": "Department: University of West Alabama: Financial Affairs: Information Technology Location: University of West Alabama Provide first-level hardware and software support for client systems, including, but not limited to: Set up and installation of new systems Diagnose and repair hardware/software issues Hardware/software configuration recommendations Maintaining classroom equipment Perform installation and maintenance for various equipment, such as : Audio/visual systems Staff workstations Printers, copiers, and other peripheral devices Other supported university IT equipment Provide professional user support : Assist users with day-to-day computer-related problems Maintain excellent customer service relationships Properly document all work in an organized manner FSA: Non-Exempt Before hiring, the final candidate(s) must successfully pass a pre-employment background investigation, including information obtained from social media and other internet sources. Required: Minimum of 1 year of experience in a technical support or customer service role. Strong knowledge of Windows operating systems and hardware/software troubleshooting. Ability to work independently and prioritize multiple tasks. Strong communication skills, both verbal and written. Attention to detail and excellent problem-solving abilities. Dedication to maintaining high standards of customer service. Desired: Bachelor’s degree in Information Technology, Computer Science, or a related field. Familiarity with audio/visual systems and networking principles. Additional IT education or IT industry certifications. To Apply :  When you select Apply Now, you will be prompted to provide the following: Cover Letter. A current resume. Transcripts of all college work.  Unofficial transcripts will suffice for application.  Official transcripts must be submitted immediately upon employment. Email and phone number of three professional references; reference letters may be requested later."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/185582": {
      "title": "Marketing Communications Coordinator for Advancement",
      "description": "Department: University of West Alabama: Communications and Marketing Location: University of West Alabama The Marketing Communications Coordinator for Advancement serves as the primary communications liaison between the Office of Communications and Marketing and the Division of Institutional Advancement, including the Offices of Alumni Affairs and Development/Giving. This position supports the development and implementation of communications that promote alumni engagement, philanthropic giving, and donor stewardship. Working within a centralized communications team, the coordinator collaborates with advancement staff and communications colleagues to produce content and coordinate projects that align with the University’s brand, messaging, and strategic priorities. Duties and Responsibilities Serve as the primary communications contact for Institutional Advancement, working closely with its staff in Alumni Affairs and Development/Giving to support communications needs and priorities. Develop and edit content for print and digital communications, including alumni and donor profiles, impact stories, email communications, newsletters, website content, and event promotion materials. Support communications related to alumni engagement, events, fundraising initiatives, and donor recognition efforts. Coordinate communications projects from planning through completion, including managing timelines, gathering content, and facilitating review and approval processes. Collaborate with members of the communications and marketing team to execute projects and ensure alignment with institutional messaging and branding. Utilize communications and marketing tools, including email marketing platforms, content management systems, social media tools, analytics platforms, and advancement systems such as Blackbaud Raiser’s Edge NXT, to support the delivery of communications. Assist in supporting targeted or segmented communications in coordination with advancement staff, as appropriate. Contribute to broader communications and marketing initiatives and perform other duties as assigned. Required Qualifications Bachelor’s degree in communications, marketing, journalism, public relations, or a related field Minimum 2 years demonstrated success in communications, marketing, public relations, or a related field Strong writing, editing, and organizational skills Ability to manage multiple projects and meet deadlines Ability to work collaboratively with a variety of stakeholders Preferred Qualifications Experience in higher education, nonprofit, or related environment Experience supporting communications related to alumni engagement, fundraising, or external relations Familiarity with digital communications platforms and content management systems Familiarity with Blackbaud Raiser’s Edge NXT or similar CRM systems Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Strong written and verbal communication skills Ability to develop content for a variety of audiences Strong attention to detail and organizational skills Ability to manage multiple priorities in a deadline-driven environment Proficiency with standard communications and marketing tools Understanding of how communications support engagement and institutional goals FLSA : Exempt Before hiring, the final candidate(s) must successfully pass a pre-employment background investigation, including information obtained from social media and other internet sources. To Apply: When you select \"Apply Now,\" you will be prompted to provide the following: Cover Letter. A current resume or CV. Transcripts of all college work. Unofficial transcripts will suffice for application.  Official transcripts must be submitted immediately upon employment. Email and phone number of three professional references.  Reference letters may be requested later."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189042": {
      "title": "Director of Communications",
      "description": "Department: University of West Alabama: Communications and Marketing Location: University of West Alabama The Director of Communications provides leadership for the University's communications function, overseeing institutional messaging, content strategy, internal communications, editorial planning, and reputation management. This role is responsible for ensuring that University communications are accurate, consistent, timely, and aligned with institutional priorities while fostering meaningful engagement with internal and external audiences. Working collaboratively with the Director of Marketing, the Director of Communications provides strategic guidance on institutional messaging and content development while ensuring that communications efforts support the University's mission, values, and strategic objectives. Duties and Responsibilities Lead the development and implementation of the University's communications strategy, ensuring alignment with institutional priorities and executive leadership objectives. Serve as the University's primary authority on institutional messaging, voice, tone, and communication standards. Support public relations efforts to ensure alignment across platforms and audiences. Provide strategic counsel and communications guidance to senior leadership regarding institutional initiatives, sensitive issues, and matters that may affect the University's reputation or public perception. Oversee the planning, development, and distribution of institutional communications across internal and external channels. Direct the University's editorial strategy, content planning, and storytelling efforts to ensure consistent communication of institutional priorities, accomplishments, and impact. Provide leadership for internal communications initiatives that support employee engagement, organizational awareness, institutional alignment, and effective dissemination of information. Oversee communications related to major institutional milestones, academic initiatives, commencement activities, University-wide announcements, and other strategic communications priorities. Collaborate with Academic Affairs, Student Affairs, Enrollment Management, Institutional Advancement, Athletics, and other University divisions to ensure effective communication and alignment of messaging across the institution. Partner with the Director of Marketing to ensure consistent application of institutional brand standards, messaging frameworks, and audience engagement strategies. Establish editorial standards, communication workflows, and content governance practices to improve consistency, responsiveness, and effectiveness across internal and external communications. Monitor emerging issues, communication challenges, and reputational considerations and provide recommendations to university leadership regarding appropriate communication strategies. Supervise and develop assigned personnel, fostering a culture of collaboration, accountability, innovation, and service excellence. Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree in Communications, Journalism, Public Relations, English, Marketing, or a related field. Five (5) years of progressively responsible professional experience in communications, public relations, journalism, content development, marketing communications, or a related field. Two (2) years of experience leading projects, teams, programs, communication initiatives, or organizational efforts. Demonstrated experience developing and implementing communication strategies, content initiatives, or public-facing communications. Experience collaborating with multiple stakeholders and managing competing priorities in a complex organizational environment. Strong written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, including the ability to communicate effectively with diverse audiences. Preferred Qualifications Master's degree in Communications, Public Relations, Journalism, Marketing, Higher Education Administration, Business Administration, or a related field. Seven (7) or more years of progressively responsible professional experience in communications, public relations, journalism, content strategy, marketing communications, or related areas. Three (3) or more years of supervisory or leadership experience with responsibility for personnel, strategic initiatives, communication planning, or organizational programs. Experience in higher education communications, public relations, media relations, journalism, or nonprofit communications. Experience advising senior leadership or supporting executive communications. Experience developing editorial strategies, content governance practices, or institutional messaging frameworks. Experience managing communication efforts related to organizational change, strategic initiatives, crisis communications, or reputation management. Experience utilizing communication metrics, audience insights, or engagement data to inform planning and decision-making. FLSA : Exempt Before hiring, the final candidate(s) must successfully pass a pre-employment background investigation, including information obtained from social media and other internet sources. To Apply: When you select \"Apply Now,\" you will be prompted to provide the following: Cover Letter. A current resume or CV. Transcripts of all college work. Unofficial transcripts will suffice for application.  Official transcripts must be submitted immediately upon employment. Email and phone number of three professional references.  Reference letters may be requested later."
