    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193987": {
      "title": "Police Officer, UWA Police Department",
      "description": "Department: University of West Alabama: Administration and Human Resources: Campus Safety and Security Location: University of West Alabama Weekend work and shift work are required, as well as regular and predictable attendance. Police Officers are directly responsible for delivering public safety and law enforcement services on and near the campus and other properties owned, controlled, or occupied by the University. Police Officers report directly to the Chief of Police and must work independently as well as collaboratively, carrying out instructions and self-initiating activity in a manner consistent with law, regulations, department policies, and standard procedures. Patrol campus on foot, in police vehicles, or other means to deter crime, direct traffic, provide service or assistance to employees or visitors to campus, and enforce traffic and parking regulations. Investigate disturbances, suspicious circumstances, traffic accidents, or violations of university regulations when prompted by phone calls, alarms, citizens’ requests, or officers’ observations. Talk with individuals to determine the causes of disturbances or accidents. Take reports and complete forms to record incidents. Direct traffic at university events. Assist local law enforcement agencies when called to enforce state laws and city ordinances. Attend and testify in municipal, circuit, district, juvenile courts, and before a grand jury when necessary. Complete police documents such as daily activity reports, traffic accident reports, permissions to search, advice of rights, incident/offense reports, arrest reports, bond forms, complaint forms, fingerprint cards, and warrants of arrest to record activity on shift, complete arrests, and investigate traffic accidents or other incidents of law violations. Provide a positive customer service experience requiring a high level of diplomacy and professionalism. Perform other related work as assigned. Salary: Dependent upon credentials; includes excellent medical and retirement benefits. Prior to hiring, the final candidate(s) must successfully pass a pre-employment background investigation, including information obtained from social media and other internet sources. Essential: High school diploma or equivalent. Good moral character and reputation. Must be eligible to attend an Alabama Peace Officer Standards and Training Commission (A.P.O.S.T.C.) academy. Must be eligible to be licensed to carry firearms. Must be at least 21 years of age and possess a valid Alabama Driver’s License. Must be a U.S. Citizen. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with Police Department personnel, other UWA employees, outside law enforcement agencies, and the public. Ability to meet attendance schedule with dependability and consistency. Ability to speak clearly and concisely. Excellent decision-making capabilities in emergency and non-emergency situations. High level of confidentiality. Strong reading comprehension skills, with the ability to follow written and oral directions. Basic computer skills, including word processing and Microsoft Outlook; ability to learn new programs. Desired: Certified police officer by the Alabama Peace Officers’ Standards Training Commission. Knowledge of police methods, practices, and procedures, and the ability to apply this knowledge to specific situations. Extensive knowledge of public safety activity and methods. Extensive knowledge of law enforcement computer technology and equipment. Extensive knowledge of law enforcement terminology and procedures. Skill in reading and interpreting maps to determine locations and jurisdictional boundaries. Before hiring, the final candidate must successfully pass a background investigation, including information obtained from social media and other internet sources. To Apply: When you select \"Apply Now,\" you will be prompted to provide the following: Cover Letter. A current resume or CV. Transcripts of all college work. Unofficial transcripts will suffice for application.  Official transcripts must be submitted immediately upon employment. Email and phone number of three professional references.  Reference letters may be requested later."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194808": {
      "title": "Director of Emergency and Risk Management",
      "description": "Department: University of West Alabama: Administration and Human Resources: Campus Safety and Security Location: University of West Alabama The Director of Emergency and Risk Management provides strategic leadership, planning, coordination, and oversight for the University of West Alabama’s comprehensive emergency management, continuity of operations, and risk management programs. This position is responsible for developing, implementing, and maintaining policies, procedures, training, and response frameworks that safeguard students, faculty, staff, visitors, facilities, and University operations. The Director ensures institutional preparedness, regulatory compliance, and risk mitigation through collaboration with University leadership and internal and external partners. Emergency Management & Preparedness Maintain and update the University’s Disaster Management Plan, Emergency Response Plan, and Continuity of Operations Plan (COOP) in alignment with FEMA’s National Incident Management System (NIMS) and Incident Command System (ICS). Lead University preparedness initiatives, including hazard identification, vulnerability assessments, and risk analyses. Coordinate emergency response planning for natural disasters, public health emergencies, active threat incidents, severe weather, infrastructure failures, and other crises. Serve as a key member of the Critical Incident Management Team, the COOP Response Team, and advise University leadership during emergencies. Conduct drills, tabletop exercises, and full-scale exercises; evaluate outcomes and implement improvements. Risk Management Lead the University’s risk management framework to identify, assess, prioritize, and mitigate institutional risks. Develop risk mitigation strategies and monitor risk indicators. Develop and maintain risk policies and procedures aligned with industry best practices. Develop risk mitigation strategies surrounding minors on campus. Training Provide training and awareness programs for faculty, staff, and students on emergency preparedness and risk mitigation. Maintain required documentation, reporting, and regulatory submissions. Partnerships & Coordination Serve as primary liaison to local, state, and federal emergency management agencies. Coordinate with law enforcement, fire services, public health agencies, and community partners. Represent the University in regional emergency planning committees and mutual aid networks. Leadership & Administration Manage departmental budget. Develop annual goals, metrics, and reports for University leadership. Advise University leadership on institutional risk posture and emergency preparedness readiness. Required: Minimum of 10 years of progressively responsible professional experience in public safety, law enforcement, emergency management, risk management, business continuity, occupational safety, or a closely related area. Working knowledge of the National Incident Management System (NIMS) and Incident Command System (ICS). Experience coordinating with law enforcement, fire and rescue services, emergency medical services, public health agencies, emergency management agencies, and other governmental or community partners. Experience with emergency notification systems, emergency operations centers, crisis communications, or emergency management technology. Ability to identify and assess risks and develop practical risk mitigation strategies. Experience managing budgets, contracts, or departmental resources. Strong organizational, analytical, problem-solving, and decision-making skills. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to communicate effectively with senior leadership, employees, students, public safety personnel, and external agencies. Demonstrated ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with internal and external stakeholders. Ability to exercise sound judgment and maintain composure when responding to emergencies, crises, and other time-sensitive situations. Ability to work independently, manage multiple priorities, and coordinate projects involving multiple departments or agencies. Ability to respond to University emergencies outside normal business hours, including evenings, weekends, and holidays, as necessary. Valid driver’s license and ability to travel as required for University business. Preferred: Fifteen or more years of progressively responsible experience in public safety, law enforcement, emergency management, risk management, business continuity, occupational safety, or a closely related area. Experience developing, implementing, or maintaining emergency response plans, continuity of operations plans, risk assessments, or related policies and procedures. Experience developing policies, procedures, training programs, and compliance documentation. Experience advising executive leadership regarding emergency preparedness, organizational risk, or crisis response. FLSA: Exempt Position Details: Review of applications will begin immediately, with an anticipated start date of February 1, 2027. Before hiring, the final candidate(s) must successfully pass a pre-employment background investigation, including information obtained from social media and other internet sources. To Apply: When you select \"Apply Now,\" you will be prompted to provide the following: Cover Letter. A current resume or CV. Transcripts of all college work. Unofficial transcripts will suffice for application.  Official transcripts must be submitted immediately upon employment. Email and phone number of three professional references.  Reference letters may be requested later."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/186878": {
      "title": "Groundskeeper",
      "description": "Department: University of West Alabama: Financial Affairs: Physical Plant Location: University of West Alabama Under the supervision of the Grounds Manager for The University of West Alabama, this position will be responsible for, but not limited to, the following: Mow, line trim, water, seed, fertilize, and maintain lawns. Prune and transplant shrubs, spread bark, and maintain flower beds as needed. Pick up trash and other related debris from all landscaped beds as needed. Keep all mulched/landscaped beds free of undesired growth. During inclement weather, some snow and ice removal may be needed. Empty trash containers and replenish bags. Some weekend work will be required. Any other duties as assigned by your supervisor. Physical Demands and Working Conditions Transporting moderately heavy items at times. Ability to bend, stoop, kneel, climb, and stand for extended periods. Performing work outside during all times of the year. Operation of University vehicles and grounds equipment. There may be a high level of walking required in this position. Required: Ability to read, write, and follow oral and written instructions. Ability to interact professionally with all members of the University community and external public. Meet the physical demands of housekeeping (see below). Valid Driver’s License. Dependable transportation. Prior to hiring, the final candidate(s) must successfully pass a pre-employment background investigation, including information obtained from social media and other internet sources. To Apply: When you select \"Apply Now,\" you will be prompted to provide the following: A current resume or CV. Email and phone number of three professional references.  Reference letters may be requested later."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194345": {
      "title": "Financial Aid Counselor/Receptionist",
      "description": "Department: University of West Alabama: Enrollment Management and Student Success: Financial Aid Location: University of West Alabama The Financial Aid Assistant is responsible for providing financial aid services to students, resolving student problems with financial aid issues, and providing application information for scholarships, grants, and other financial aid programs. This position will ensure student understanding of policies and procedures related to financial aid. Responsibilities : Within the scope of college policies and procedures, this position: Performs exceptional customer service for students, employees, and the public by responding to inquiries concerning financial aid eligibility while ensuring students understand all policies and procedures related to their financial aid records. Responsible for counseling and advising students and parents on the Financial Aid process. Awards student aid based on independent assessment of students' eligibility needs and interprets and modifies computer-generated award packages as needed. Maintain the front office area while modeling excellent customer service, organizational details, and professionalism. Maintain all Zoom Zendesk operations regarding answering phone calls and tickets from One Stop. Responsible for responding to the needs of students, the general public, and staff by coordinating all communication with the financial aid email account. Responsible for handling all visitors/students for “on-demand/walk-in” student aid counseling. Responsible for retrieving, opening, processing, date entering, and routing all incoming mail. Provides general information to prospective and returning students regarding the financial aid application process and federal, state, and institutional guidelines. Knowledge of federal, state, and local laws, regulations, and guidelines related to assigned work. Participates and collaborates in outreach activities and special events for the Financial Aid Office. Will be required to have knowledge of the financial aid processes and counseling techniques utilized by our institution. Cross-trains with other staff as backup for all financial aid processes. Any additional duties/responsibilities assigned by the Director of Financial Aid. FLSA: Non-Exempt Required: Bachelor’s Degree from an accredited University. A minimum of one year full-time equivalent experience related to the duties described for this position. Experience with Microsoft Office. Other Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities : Ability to manage time effectively, set priorities, resolve problems independently, and make informed, appropriate decisions. Ability to work independently and collaboratively as part of a team. Strong written and verbal skills, with attention to detail. Ability to handle stressful situations with professionalism. Ability to analyze problems, identify solutions, and take appropriate action to resolve problems using independent judgment and decision-making processes. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with other departmental staff, faculty, students, and the public. Before hiring, the final candidate(s) must successfully pass a pre-employment background investigation, including information obtained from social media and other internet sources. To Apply: When you select \"Apply Now,\" you will be prompted to provide the following: Cover Letter. A current resume or CV. Transcripts of all college work. Unofficial transcripts will suffice for application.  Official transcripts must be submitted immediately upon employment. Email and phone number of three professional references.  Reference letters may be requested later."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/181128": {
      "title": "Graduate Hall Director",
      "description": "Department: University of West Alabama: Student Affairs: Housing and Residence Life Location: University of West Alabama Under the supervision of the Director of Residence Life, the Graduate Hall Director (GHD) is a ten-month live-on member of the Residence Life staff responsible for promoting and maintaining a positive living environment. The GHD position offers experiences in leadership, supervision, advising, mediation, conduct management, crisis response, budgeting, and facilities. The GHD is responsible for the day-to-day management of a residence hall or apartment area. In addition, the GHD assists with department-wide initiatives, such as staff selection and training. The GHD has on-call responsibilities and must respond quickly to emergencies. The GHD is deemed essential staff and may be required to report to work when the University is closed for emergencies and inclement weather. The GHD position is for one academic year and is renewable for up to two (2) additional years based on job performance and continued academic progress toward degree completion. Summer housing may also be available. Responsibilities: Complete up to 29 hours per week in direct job-related functions. This includes 15 hours of office hours, plus time for staff meetings and department initiatives. The typical week will require approximately 20 hours. Train, supervise, and evaluate Resident Assistants. Coordinate programming and community development efforts within the hall and manage the programming budget for the residence hall. Serve as a hearing officer, making appropriate disciplinary referrals as needed. Participate in the emergency on-call rotation within Housing & Residence Life and respond to and resolve emergency and non-emergency situations. Provide crisis intervention, conflict mediation, academic and general counseling, and resource referrals for residents and Resident Assistants. Develop a positive working relationship with the custodial and maintenance staff to support cleaning and repairs in the residence hall. Coordinate at least one department-wide function (RA training, Room Selection, etc.). Other related duties as assigned. Compensation: Furnished apartment with cable, internet, and all other utilities included. A scholarship equal to the cost of tuition for 6 credit hours plus the Tiger One fee each semester (Fall and Spring). Meal plan while classes are in session. $500 per month stipend. Reserved parking space on campus; GHD is responsible for registering, paying for, and obtaining a parking decal to park on campus in order to use the reserved parking space. Required: An earned Bachelor’s degree from a regionally accredited college/university. Undergraduate UWA students in their last semester of college with significant experience in housing and residence life will also be considered. Admitted as a graduate student and enrolled as a graduate student in at least two classes for a full academic term (Fall or Spring) or one class per online term (Fall 1, Fall 2, Spring 1, Spring 2). Demonstrated leadership skills, including the ability to communicate effectively both orally and in writing, and the desire to work with students, both individually and in groups. Preferred: Prior experience as a Resident Assistant, or related position. Experience coordinating and/or presenting training workshops. Crisis response experience. Other Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities: Strong written and verbal skills, with an attention to detail and superior organizational skills. Must live in the assigned residence hall apartment. Ability to hear and speak clearly and concisely, both face-to-face and on the phone. Specific vision abilities required by this position include close vision and distance vision. Ability to manage time effectively, set priorities, resolve problems independently, and make decisions. Ability to sit, stand, walk, climb stairs, use hands to feel, reach with hands and arms, smell, and lift and/or move items up to 10 pounds. Ability to occasionally stoop, kneel, crouch, crawl, and lift and/or move items up to 25 pounds. Some accommodations are possible, but the GHD must be able to climb stairs to access residence hall rooms in buildings without elevators. Before hiring, the final candidate(s) must successfully pass a pre-employment background investigation, including information obtained from social media and other internet sources. To Apply: When you select \"Apply Now,\" you will be prompted to provide the following: Cover Letter. A current resume or CV. Transcripts of all college work. Unofficial transcripts will suffice for application.  Official transcripts must be submitted immediately upon employment. Email and phone number of three professional references.  Reference letters may be requested later."
    }
  },
